  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 259 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will shift eastward over the ArkLaTex and\nMississippi Valley region on Tuesday as southwesterly flow aloft\ndevelops over our forecast area in advance of the next trough\nlifting over the western CONUS/Four Corners. The large area of \nsurface high pressure will also shift eastward toward the Ohio\nValley and Eastern Seaboard. A southeasterly to southerly surface\nflow pattern will prevail over our region on Tuesday and the moist\nlow level flow may keep low cloud decks in place through much of\nthe day over southeast New Mexico into the Permian Basin and \nLower Trans Pecos, where highs may only reach into the mid to\nupper 50s. The southwesterly flow pattern aloft looks to prevail\nthrough the middle to latter part of the week ahead of a slow\nmoving trough over the western U.S. Weak shortwave impulses in the\nsouthwesterly flow aloft along with increased southerly low level\nflow/moist advection may supporting increasing cloud cover and a\nslight chance of rain showers and thunderstorms over portions of\nour area Thursday and Friday. The upper-level trough looks to\ndampen as it swings into the central Plains by Saturday. This may\nallow the moisture plume to shift further to our east by early\nnext weekend. Temperatures otherwise look to trend warmer\nWednesday-Saturday, with highs moderating back into the 60s and\n70s, while morning lows mostly range in the 40s and 50s.",
